1
00:00:00,767 --> 00:00:02,668
<i>- Previously on</i> Gotham...
- Everybody,

2
00:00:03,070 --> 00:00:05,403
raise your glasses to the killer

3
00:00:05,539 --> 00:00:07,531
of Jeremiah Valeska.

4
00:00:07,674 --> 00:00:09,006
(cheering)

5
00:00:09,142 --> 00:00:10,542
A lead on a guy selling RPGs.

6
00:00:10,677 --> 00:00:12,077
Get out of here, Barbara.

7
00:00:21,355 --> 00:00:22,355
(gunshot)

8
00:00:26,093 --> 00:00:26,617
Eduardo?

9
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:28,251
Nice shot, right?

10
00:00:30,063 --> 00:00:31,429
Tell me what you did!

11
00:00:31,565 --> 00:00:33,295
I put a chip in your brain.

12
00:00:33,433 --> 00:00:34,433
Better talk, now.

13
00:00:34,468 --> 00:00:36,061
A contract came along.

14
00:00:36,203 --> 00:00:38,069
A contract from who?

15
00:00:38,205 --> 00:00:39,264
(beeps)

16
00:00:39,406 --> 00:00:42,069
Walker wants Ed
Nygma taken out now.

17
00:00:42,209 --> 00:00:43,336
Walker was behind this?

18
00:00:43,477 --> 00:00:45,241
She's been controlling
Nygma the whole time?

19
00:00:49,750 --> 00:00:51,309
- (gasps)
- Ouch.

20
00:00:51,451 --> 00:00:53,044
Stitches still sore, huh?

21
00:00:53,186 --> 00:00:56,054
I told you I had to let
Selina thrust the knife

22
00:00:56,189 --> 00:00:58,522
into my flesh at least once.

23
00:00:58,759 --> 00:00:59,522
(beeps)

24
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:00,753
You know Jim Gordon.

25
00:01:01,094 --> 00:01:02,118
You know this city.

26
00:01:02,462 --> 00:01:03,760
Find him. And kill him.

27
00:01:10,337 --> 00:01:12,306
(panting)

28
00:01:40,734 --> 00:01:42,498
(gunfire)

29
00:01:45,472 --> 00:01:46,667
(grunts)

30
00:01:48,575 --> 00:01:51,704
Your chances of survival
are astronomically low, James.

31
00:01:54,281 --> 00:01:56,546
Continuing to
run is ill-advised.

32
00:02:13,367 --> 00:02:14,767
(door opens)

33
00:02:37,257 --> 00:02:38,384
(thudding)

34
00:02:44,765 --> 00:02:46,666
(whirring)

35
00:02:55,275 --> 00:02:57,301
♪♪

36
00:03:03,450 --> 00:03:05,578
Hope you're well rested, Edward.

37
00:03:06,386 --> 00:03:08,821
We have a lot of
work ahead of us.

38
00:03:11,591 --> 00:03:12,354
(sighs)

39
00:03:12,492 --> 00:03:14,757
Edward? (chuckles)

40
00:03:16,296 --> 00:03:17,525
(sniffing)

41
00:03:17,731 --> 00:03:20,291
Edward, what is it?

42
00:03:21,435 --> 00:03:22,960
Is someone down there, boy?

43
00:03:24,638 --> 00:03:26,231
(Edward whimpers)

44
00:03:26,707 --> 00:03:28,335
MAGPIE: ♪ Cash
and jewels aplenty ♪

45
00:03:28,475 --> 00:03:29,875
♪ Sparkle in the sky ♪

46
00:03:30,210 --> 00:03:31,508
♪ Five and ten and 20 ♪

47
00:03:31,645 --> 00:03:33,773
♪ Time to say bye-bye. ♪

48
00:03:34,414 --> 00:03:36,576
Ooh, pretty, pretty.

49
00:03:36,717 --> 00:03:39,778
I'll call you
Charlotte. (giggles)

50
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:43,322
Ooh, you're a handsome
devil. What's your name?

51
00:03:43,957 --> 00:03:45,858
Oswald Cobblepot.

52
00:03:46,426 --> 00:03:48,759
But I'm guessing
you already knew that.

53
00:03:57,404 --> 00:03:58,404
Stop!

54
00:04:03,844 --> 00:04:06,609
Penguin. Didn't think
you'd miss a couple things.

55
00:04:06,747 --> 00:04:08,579
Couple things go
missing all the time.

56
00:04:08,915 --> 00:04:10,577
How did you know
about this place?

57
00:04:11,284 --> 00:04:12,843
All of the thieves
I used are dead.

58
00:04:12,986 --> 00:04:14,386
Yep, yep.

59
00:04:14,955 --> 00:04:16,321
Was on a job.

60
00:04:16,456 --> 00:04:17,924
Someone got there first.

61
00:04:18,358 --> 00:04:19,656
Followed them back here.

62
00:04:19,793 --> 00:04:21,694
Saw you. Bang, bang.

63
00:04:23,029 --> 00:04:25,294
Then you know what is in store.

64
00:04:25,832 --> 00:04:28,961
I do hope whatever you came
here for was worth your life.

65
00:04:31,304 --> 00:04:33,432
(both laugh)

66
00:04:33,774 --> 00:04:34,833
The Russifer Diamond.

67
00:04:34,975 --> 00:04:38,742
Not Russifer. Charlotte.

68
00:04:39,646 --> 00:04:40,875
What's in your other hand?

69
00:04:43,984 --> 00:04:45,953
Wait, how did you...

70
00:04:50,624 --> 00:04:51,421
(groans)

71
00:04:51,558 --> 00:04:52,651
Bye-bye.

72
00:04:56,363 --> 00:04:58,332
(panting)

73
00:05:02,969 --> 00:05:06,428
Who the hell was that?

74
00:05:08,008 --> 00:05:10,842
(grunts) Oh, God...

75
00:05:10,977 --> 00:05:12,639
Oh! (panting)

76
00:05:12,879 --> 00:05:15,439
Oh, God. It happened
again, didn't it?

77
00:05:15,982 --> 00:05:16,982
What did I do?

78
00:05:17,017 --> 00:05:18,576
You tried to kill me.

79
00:05:18,919 --> 00:05:21,582
Oh. Is that all?

80
00:05:21,888 --> 00:05:23,481
I need answers, Ed.

81
00:05:23,857 --> 00:05:25,758
I know Walker gave the
orders to destroy Haven

82
00:05:25,892 --> 00:05:27,588
and the supply helicopter.

83
00:05:27,894 --> 00:05:29,362
What is she planning next?

84
00:05:29,496 --> 00:05:30,496
I don't know.

85
00:05:31,932 --> 00:05:35,460
Jim, I don't remember
anything once I go under.

86
00:05:36,503 --> 00:05:37,562
You're bleeding.

87
00:05:40,907 --> 00:05:43,672
You have to let me go.
I'm not responsible for this.

88
00:05:44,477 --> 00:05:46,077
That chip in your head
could be evidence.

89
00:05:46,446 --> 00:05:47,846
So unless you
want me to rip it out,

90
00:05:48,448 --> 00:05:50,508
- you're coming
with me.
- Okay.

91
00:05:50,650 --> 00:05:51,515
(grunts)

92
00:05:51,651 --> 00:05:53,552
(gunfire in distance)

93
00:05:57,090 --> 00:05:59,059
(indistinct chatter)

94
00:06:03,730 --> 00:06:04,730
ALFRED: Bruce.

95
00:06:04,865 --> 00:06:05,865
There you are.

96
00:06:07,467 --> 00:06:09,595
So, go on, then.

97
00:06:10,737 --> 00:06:11,966
How'd it go with Selina?

98
00:06:13,106 --> 00:06:13,835
Oh, dear.

99
00:06:13,974 --> 00:06:16,603
Well, I am sorry, Master Bruce,
but perhaps it's for the best.

100
00:06:16,743 --> 00:06:19,770
Maybe. That's not why I
called you here. You see this?

101
00:06:20,046 --> 00:06:22,515
Yeah, I know, right?
Looks like Captain Gordon's

102
00:06:22,649 --> 00:06:24,948
finally getting the help this
city so desperately needs.

103
00:06:25,552 --> 00:06:26,576
Is he?

104
00:06:26,987 --> 00:06:29,098
What the people desperately
need is food and medicine.

105
00:06:29,122 --> 00:06:30,488
Not just more guns.

106
00:06:32,759 --> 00:06:33,759
What is it?

107
00:06:34,594 --> 00:06:35,755
No, it's just this unit.

108
00:06:35,896 --> 00:06:37,956
Their motto is "Born of Blood."

109
00:06:42,068 --> 00:06:43,161
You know them?

110
00:06:43,703 --> 00:06:44,703
Yeah, by reputation only.

111
00:06:44,738 --> 00:06:46,178
They're a horrible
bunch of bastards.

112
00:06:46,573 --> 00:06:49,441
They specialize in military
coups, assassinations.

113
00:06:49,576 --> 00:06:50,896
They're certainly
not the vanguard

114
00:06:50,944 --> 00:06:53,072
for humanitarian relief.

115
00:06:55,048 --> 00:06:56,107
Excuse me.

116
00:06:57,117 --> 00:06:59,018
When's the supply
shipment gonna be here?

117
00:06:59,619 --> 00:07:00,619
Supplies?

118
00:07:00,720 --> 00:07:02,450
These people are
desperate for food,

119
00:07:03,056 --> 00:07:04,820
clean water, medicine.

120
00:07:05,058 --> 00:07:07,050
Oh, yeah. It's on its way.

121
00:07:09,129 --> 00:07:10,563
Where's Captain Gordon?

122
00:07:13,667 --> 00:07:15,033
We lost sight of Gordon.

123
00:07:15,168 --> 00:07:17,899
But don't worry. Eduardo's out
there looking for him right now.

124
00:07:18,505 --> 00:07:20,906
Unless you know
where we might find him?

125
00:07:21,708 --> 00:07:23,700
Logically, he'd come here.

126
00:07:24,144 --> 00:07:26,477
Unless there's
some reason he can't.

127
00:07:28,181 --> 00:07:29,181
Tell you what, kiddo.

128
00:07:30,050 --> 00:07:31,961
I'm gonna find you and your
friend a quiet place in the back

129
00:07:31,985 --> 00:07:32,850
where you can wait for Gordon.

130
00:07:32,986 --> 00:07:34,063
Ah, don't you
worry about it, mate.

131
00:07:34,087 --> 00:07:35,612
We'll just catch
up with him later.

132
00:07:35,755 --> 00:07:36,882
I insist.

133
00:07:54,808 --> 00:07:56,106
We need to find Jim.

134
00:07:56,710 --> 00:07:59,578
So the government is behind
Haven and the supply helicopter,

135
00:07:59,713 --> 00:08:01,875
and they did it with a
chip in this one's head?

136
00:08:02,082 --> 00:08:03,225
Is that really
the craziest thing

137
00:08:03,249 --> 00:08:04,329
you've ever heard, Barbara?

138
00:08:04,684 --> 00:08:05,761
Bruce hit me up on the radio

139
00:08:05,785 --> 00:08:07,129
a little while ago,
looking for you.

140
00:08:07,153 --> 00:08:08,951
Said things are
amiss in the GCPD.

141
00:08:09,622 --> 00:08:11,887
I'll reach out, see if we can
meet up somewhere safe.

142
00:08:12,125 --> 00:08:14,203
Can you pull in some of your
people, see if they can help?

143
00:08:14,227 --> 00:08:16,219
No. I need them
guarding my territory.

144
00:08:16,963 --> 00:08:17,760
But you have me.

145
00:08:17,931 --> 00:08:18,931
And me.

146
00:08:19,165 --> 00:08:20,224
I don't trust you.

147
00:08:20,900 --> 00:08:22,420
With or without that
chip in your head.

148
00:08:22,569 --> 00:08:23,569
Jim.

149
00:08:23,770 --> 00:08:26,797
They used me as a pawn
to murder innocent people.

150
00:08:26,940 --> 00:08:29,000
They messed with my brain.

151
00:08:29,209 --> 00:08:31,610
The thing that I
want is revenge.

152
00:08:32,012 --> 00:08:34,982
How do I know that
chip can't be reactivated?

153
00:08:35,949 --> 00:08:37,226
Well, when you found
me at Strange's lab,

154
00:08:37,250 --> 00:08:39,617
he was fixing it after a...

155
00:08:40,286 --> 00:08:41,811
car battery mishap...

156
00:08:41,955 --> 00:08:44,618
I-It's safe to say that I
think you shorted it out.

157
00:08:45,725 --> 00:08:46,725
Let me look at that.

158
00:08:47,027 --> 00:08:48,757
- What?
- Your neck.

159
00:08:53,666 --> 00:08:56,659
That's gonna need
stitches. I have a first aid kit.

160
00:08:57,037 --> 00:08:59,802
I need to keep moving.
Just name your fee.

161
00:09:00,540 --> 00:09:01,580
Can't I just want to help?

162
00:09:02,075 --> 00:09:03,771
Not you, not usually.

163
00:09:04,878 --> 00:09:06,055
Friend of mine
just tried to kill me.

164
00:09:06,079 --> 00:09:07,690
I don't want anyone
else waiting for an excuse

165
00:09:07,714 --> 00:09:09,859
- to stab me in the back.
- I gave you
Gerry the Jerkwad,

166
00:09:09,883 --> 00:09:11,545
and I'm letting you
hide out in my club.

167
00:09:11,684 --> 00:09:14,017
If I wanted to betray you, I
would have done it earlier.

168
00:09:14,254 --> 00:09:15,813
So, where's that first aid kit?

169
00:09:18,591 --> 00:09:20,560
Gonna make myself useful.

170
00:09:21,795 --> 00:09:23,661
What's it gonna take
for you to trust me?

171
00:09:24,697 --> 00:09:28,327
After everything you've
done, I honestly don't know.

172
00:09:29,135 --> 00:09:30,967
EDUARDO: I'd listen to her, Jim.

173
00:09:33,339 --> 00:09:37,037
I hear trust is in short supply.

174
00:09:43,716 --> 00:09:46,208
EDUARDO: You forget
your PSYOPS training, Jim?

175
00:09:46,619 --> 00:09:49,316
Lesson one: when
a person is in trouble,

176
00:09:49,756 --> 00:09:51,247
they go to a familiar place.

177
00:09:52,158 --> 00:09:55,026
Your ex-fiancée's bar was
just a stone's throw away,

178
00:09:55,195 --> 00:09:57,994
so it's nice to finally
meet you, Barbara.

179
00:09:58,331 --> 00:09:59,331
Screw you.

180
00:09:59,766 --> 00:10:00,766
Hmm.

181
00:10:02,302 --> 00:10:04,328
It seems you've lost your tail.

182
00:10:05,338 --> 00:10:06,829
You mean the psychopath

183
00:10:06,973 --> 00:10:08,271
you ordered to kill me?

184
00:10:09,275 --> 00:10:10,937
You had your chance.

185
00:10:11,845 --> 00:10:13,905
It could have been
like old times, buddy.

186
00:10:14,047 --> 00:10:15,379
You, me, together,

187
00:10:16,015 --> 00:10:18,280
making the world a better place.

188
00:10:18,852 --> 00:10:21,754
- By murdering civilians?
- Civilians?

189
00:10:22,122 --> 00:10:25,115
This hellhole is infested
with criminals and terrorists.

190
00:10:25,658 --> 00:10:27,786
Sometimes you just have
to clean house, you know?

191
00:10:28,027 --> 00:10:29,154
I won't let you.

192
00:10:29,729 --> 00:10:31,027
It's not your call, Jim.

193
00:10:31,164 --> 00:10:32,792
(guns clicking)

194
00:10:33,199 --> 00:10:34,724
I'm not dead yet.

195
00:10:37,704 --> 00:10:40,037
James Gordon, I've
been ordered to kill you.

196
00:10:40,173 --> 00:10:42,802
Well, looks like you
haven't lost that tail after all.

197
00:10:51,684 --> 00:10:52,684
(straining): If I die,

198
00:10:53,386 --> 00:10:56,015
you'll never get the evidence!

199
00:10:56,856 --> 00:10:58,256
Stand down.

200
00:11:03,229 --> 00:11:08,167
I have evidence that you
and Walker are behind Haven.

201
00:11:08,968 --> 00:11:11,938
If I die, it gets out
to the entire country.

202
00:11:12,739 --> 00:11:14,298
It's time to make a deal.

203
00:11:15,308 --> 00:11:16,901
You don't have any evidence.

204
00:11:17,310 --> 00:11:19,142
Is that a risk you're
willing to take?

205
00:11:21,447 --> 00:11:22,447
It is.

206
00:11:22,949 --> 00:11:25,180
Nygma, kill them both.

207
00:11:47,407 --> 00:11:49,376
(bell tolling in distance)

208
00:11:54,881 --> 00:11:56,179
(screaming)

209
00:11:59,886 --> 00:12:01,115
(door closes)

210
00:12:10,063 --> 00:12:11,063
Bad time?

211
00:12:13,433 --> 00:12:15,197
(sighs) What do you want?

212
00:12:21,941 --> 00:12:23,170
Depends.

213
00:12:23,309 --> 00:12:24,436
What did <i>they</i> want?

214
00:12:25,411 --> 00:12:28,040
Oh. A shot at me.

215
00:12:29,082 --> 00:12:30,812
I killed Jeremiah Valeska.

216
00:12:31,484 --> 00:12:32,816
(laughs softly)

217
00:12:33,152 --> 00:12:34,745
So the rumors are true.

218
00:12:35,188 --> 00:12:36,383
Yeah.

219
00:12:37,056 --> 00:12:39,787
And now, every scumbag in
town wants to take me down

220
00:12:39,926 --> 00:12:41,451
to make a name for themselves.

221
00:12:43,863 --> 00:12:45,491
So I got to find a new squat.

222
00:12:48,334 --> 00:12:52,271
How about, in exchange
for helping me find someone,

223
00:12:53,206 --> 00:12:55,232
I let you crash in my citadel?

224
00:12:55,541 --> 00:12:57,305
It's seen better days, but...

225
00:12:57,810 --> 00:12:59,403
(laughs): then again, right?

226
00:12:59,879 --> 00:13:00,879
Who?

227
00:13:01,281 --> 00:13:03,910
A miscreant I caught in
the act of stealing from me.

228
00:13:04,384 --> 00:13:06,080
She was going to
replace the stolen item

229
00:13:06,219 --> 00:13:10,020
with an exact replica, except
the replica was a bomb.

230
00:13:10,156 --> 00:13:12,284
- (chuckles)
- Ring any bells?

231
00:13:12,458 --> 00:13:14,086
White hair, feathered collar,

232
00:13:14,227 --> 00:13:15,991
kind of of resembles
a goth chicken?

233
00:13:16,195 --> 00:13:17,322
You do know her.

234
00:13:18,031 --> 00:13:19,124
(chuckles)

235
00:13:19,499 --> 00:13:21,434
She goes by the name Magpie.

236
00:13:23,336 --> 00:13:24,336
What'd she steal?

237
00:13:25,371 --> 00:13:26,805
The Russifer Diamond.

238
00:13:27,540 --> 00:13:31,102
What makes you
think I would help you

239
00:13:32,178 --> 00:13:33,373
after you killed Tabitha

240
00:13:33,513 --> 00:13:35,072
in cold blood?

241
00:13:35,948 --> 00:13:38,144
Because you believe
in an eye for an eye.

242
00:13:39,452 --> 00:13:41,387
Hard as it may be to swallow,

243
00:13:41,988 --> 00:13:44,981
Tabitha made her bed
when she killed my mother.

244
00:13:49,429 --> 00:13:50,488
Tell you what.

245
00:13:51,564 --> 00:13:53,556
I will help you

246
00:13:54,867 --> 00:13:57,837
if I get to keep the diamond.

247
00:13:58,237 --> 00:14:01,833
(laughs)

248
00:14:01,974 --> 00:14:03,203
What?

249
00:14:06,479 --> 00:14:07,879
Not a chance.

250
00:14:08,548 --> 00:14:11,211
Ha, ha, ha. Then
you'll never find her.

251
00:14:11,951 --> 00:14:16,286
And every person in
Gotham will think it's okay

252
00:14:16,422 --> 00:14:18,254
to steal from Penguin.

253
00:14:19,292 --> 00:14:20,988
Is that what you want?

254
00:14:23,596 --> 00:14:25,030
You know me too well.

255
00:14:26,265 --> 00:14:27,324
Fine.

256
00:14:29,135 --> 00:14:30,194
Good.

257
00:14:30,503 --> 00:14:32,181
- What happened?
- EDUARDO: I want
every soldier

258
00:14:32,205 --> 00:14:33,935
in the field looking
for Jim Gordon.

259
00:14:34,207 --> 00:14:36,438
Sir, we maxed out
the holding cells.

260
00:14:36,609 --> 00:14:39,101
We may need to keep men
here to control the overflow.

261
00:14:40,279 --> 00:14:42,145
Ethan, Sumiko.

262
00:14:43,049 --> 00:14:44,312
We're moving to phase two.

263
00:14:47,987 --> 00:14:49,421
(grunts)

264
00:14:49,555 --> 00:14:50,921
What in the hell's going on?

265
00:14:51,924 --> 00:14:53,222
Listen up.

266
00:14:53,626 --> 00:14:55,492
I am not here to play nice.

267
00:14:55,895 --> 00:14:58,330
I am not here to
hold anyone's hand.

268
00:14:58,464 --> 00:15:01,059
I am here on a simple mission.

269
00:15:03,436 --> 00:15:05,905
Any questions about
the nature of that mission?

270
00:15:06,172 --> 00:15:09,438
Are you insane? You
can't just execute people.

271
00:15:10,009 --> 00:15:11,136
Do not question me.

272
00:15:11,277 --> 00:15:13,143
This is the mission.
If you don't like it,

273
00:15:13,279 --> 00:15:15,111
you'll be locked up,
you'll remain locked up,

274
00:15:15,248 --> 00:15:16,978
until we purge the
criminal element

275
00:15:17,116 --> 00:15:18,482
from the streets of Gotham.

276
00:15:19,218 --> 00:15:21,619
Sir, Secretary
Walker's on the radio.

277
00:15:21,954 --> 00:15:23,183
(cell door closes)

278
00:15:24,590 --> 00:15:26,923
We've moved on to phase
two, but we've lost track

279
00:15:27,059 --> 00:15:28,527
of Gordon and Nygma.

280
00:15:29,195 --> 00:15:30,875
I think Gordon found
a way to fry the chip.

281
00:15:31,631 --> 00:15:32,674
WALKER: We can't have Nygma

282
00:15:32,698 --> 00:15:35,031
in the wind with all that
evidence in his head.

283
00:15:35,168 --> 00:15:37,194
Just trust me.
Once we find them...

284
00:15:37,336 --> 00:15:38,463
Don't bother.

285
00:15:38,704 --> 00:15:42,641
I have an idea that will bring
Jim Gordon and Nygma to us.

286
00:15:45,178 --> 00:15:46,544
Thanks for getting
everyone here.

287
00:15:47,146 --> 00:15:48,478
And for the Wayne R and D.

288
00:15:48,948 --> 00:15:51,577
Are you sure about including
Barbara Kean and Ed Nygma?

289
00:15:52,385 --> 00:15:54,217
War makes for
strange bedfellows.

290
00:15:55,021 --> 00:15:56,432
It's good to have a
friend nearby, Bruce.

291
00:15:56,456 --> 00:15:58,721
Ow! Would you be careful?

292
00:15:59,058 --> 00:16:00,969
If you'll stop moving,
this will go a lot faster.

293
00:16:00,993 --> 00:16:02,604
NYGMA: Well, if you would be
more careful, I wouldn't potato

294
00:16:02,628 --> 00:16:04,529
all the long legs from
the hidden spoon.

295
00:16:06,165 --> 00:16:07,165
What just happened?

296
00:16:07,333 --> 00:16:09,614
I believe the chip is installed
against his speech center.

297
00:16:10,036 --> 00:16:11,629
Huh. Do it again.

298
00:16:12,271 --> 00:16:14,467
The human brain is not
a plaything, Ms. Kean.

299
00:16:16,008 --> 00:16:18,170
Please don't habituate
the curtain rod.

300
00:16:18,611 --> 00:16:19,611
(chuckles)

301
00:16:20,012 --> 00:16:22,538
And... ah.

302
00:16:23,349 --> 00:16:24,349
Stay.

303
00:16:27,153 --> 00:16:28,382
There. How does that feel?

304
00:16:28,654 --> 00:16:29,654
(grunts)

305
00:16:30,756 --> 00:16:32,520
Peter Piper picked a
peck of pickled peppers.

306
00:16:32,658 --> 00:16:35,492
Oh, God. Thank you, Lucius.

307
00:16:36,662 --> 00:16:38,563
LUCIUS: So what
exactly are we looking for?

308
00:16:39,098 --> 00:16:42,000
Anything tying Walker or
Eduardo to the destruction of Haven.

309
00:16:42,268 --> 00:16:44,032
Why would Walker
kill all those people?

310
00:16:44,270 --> 00:16:46,068
That's what we have to find out.

311
00:16:47,106 --> 00:16:48,301
Oh, yes.

312
00:16:51,577 --> 00:16:53,137
It appears the chip
retains an audio log

313
00:16:53,279 --> 00:16:56,044
of every verbal communication
with Nygma while he was under.

314
00:16:56,315 --> 00:16:58,546
There's some damage, but...

315
00:16:58,684 --> 00:17:00,448
if the command to
attack Haven is still here,

316
00:17:00,586 --> 00:17:01,697
I can trace it back
to the computer

317
00:17:01,721 --> 00:17:02,721
from which it originated.

318
00:17:03,022 --> 00:17:04,251
It has to be Walker.

319
00:17:04,790 --> 00:17:07,487
They won't be able to sweep
this under the rug anymore.

320
00:17:11,764 --> 00:17:13,528
Are you out there, Jim?

321
00:17:16,235 --> 00:17:17,396
What do you want?

322
00:17:19,305 --> 00:17:21,069
We have someone here...

323
00:17:21,641 --> 00:17:23,735
who wants to say hello.

324
00:17:28,180 --> 00:17:30,479
THOMPKINS: J-Jim?
What's happening?

325
00:17:33,052 --> 00:17:34,052
Lee?

326
00:17:39,525 --> 00:17:40,805
We found the
Queen of the Narrows

327
00:17:41,093 --> 00:17:44,154
in a run-down hidey-hole
on the north side. Tell me,

328
00:17:44,430 --> 00:17:47,457
how many ex-fiancées
do you have, anyway?

329
00:17:47,733 --> 00:17:49,373
Leave her out of this.
She's not involved.

330
00:17:49,635 --> 00:17:52,332
Ah, but she's a criminal,
and all criminals in Gotham

331
00:17:52,471 --> 00:17:54,269
are now under a death sentence.

332
00:17:55,274 --> 00:17:57,766
But what happens
next is up to you.

333
00:17:58,210 --> 00:17:59,610
A trade, perhaps.

334
00:18:00,313 --> 00:18:03,249
Lee Thompkins for Ed Nygma.

335
00:18:05,251 --> 00:18:06,378
Fine.

336
00:18:07,753 --> 00:18:10,621
Good. Bring Nygma
to the GCPD now.

337
00:18:11,324 --> 00:18:12,451
No, we meet at Haven.

338
00:18:12,592 --> 00:18:14,652
I want you to see the
carnage you've caused.

339
00:18:15,094 --> 00:18:16,494
Always so emotional.

340
00:18:16,829 --> 00:18:18,593
That's not how
wars are won, Jim.

341
00:18:18,831 --> 00:18:20,493
Those are my terms.
Take it or leave it.

342
00:18:20,633 --> 00:18:22,625
(radio crackles)

343
00:18:23,336 --> 00:18:26,329
Okay. I'll play your game.

344
00:18:29,141 --> 00:18:30,336
What are you doing?

345
00:18:30,576 --> 00:18:32,374
He's never gonna let
you walk out of there.

346
00:18:32,578 --> 00:18:35,514
I don't recall volunteering
as a trading chit!

347
00:18:35,648 --> 00:18:37,674
He doesn't want Ed.
He wants the chip.

348
00:18:37,817 --> 00:18:39,877
So I'll give him
the chip. Bruce,

349
00:18:40,419 --> 00:18:42,285
do you have any contacts
in the mainland press?

350
00:18:42,421 --> 00:18:43,421
Several.

351
00:18:43,556 --> 00:18:45,801
What would it take to broadcast
the information from the chip

352
00:18:45,825 --> 00:18:47,885
- to the mainland?
- A transmitter, which I have,

353
00:18:48,227 --> 00:18:50,423
and a very large
antenna, which I do not.

354
00:18:53,165 --> 00:18:54,743
I know where there's an
antenna in the Green Zone,

355
00:18:54,767 --> 00:18:55,927
but you're not gonna like it.

356
00:18:56,135 --> 00:18:57,797
The roof of the GCPD.

357
00:19:03,709 --> 00:19:04,709
(whispering): Lee.

358
00:19:09,749 --> 00:19:11,349
- Harvey.
- Where the hell have you been?

359
00:19:11,450 --> 00:19:12,611
You've been gone for months.

360
00:19:12,918 --> 00:19:14,147
I don't know.

361
00:19:15,221 --> 00:19:17,554
I can't remember anything.

362
00:19:17,857 --> 00:19:19,223
What happened to me?

363
00:19:19,425 --> 00:19:22,327
I don't know. We're
gonna figure it out.

364
00:19:22,595 --> 00:19:23,595
I promise.

365
00:19:23,863 --> 00:19:24,863
Let's go.

366
00:19:27,400 --> 00:19:28,732
BULLOCK: It's gonna be okay.

367
00:19:35,274 --> 00:19:36,274
(beeping, clicking)

368
00:19:36,308 --> 00:19:37,686
GORDON (over radio):
Eduardo just arrived.

369
00:19:37,710 --> 00:19:40,202
He brought backup, like
we expected. Time to move.

370
00:19:40,346 --> 00:19:41,346
Copy that.

371
00:19:42,348 --> 00:19:43,348
Let's go.

372
00:19:51,390 --> 00:19:53,450
(birds squawking)

373
00:19:57,496 --> 00:19:58,496
THOMPKINS: Jim!

374
00:19:58,564 --> 00:19:59,964
Who are these people?

375
00:20:00,499 --> 00:20:02,434
I'm gonna get you
out of here, Lee.

376
00:20:03,269 --> 00:20:04,828
I'm not sure that's true, Jim.

377
00:20:05,538 --> 00:20:07,370
It seems you forgot
to bring someone.

378
00:20:07,707 --> 00:20:10,302
It's hard to make a swap
when you have nothing to trade.

379
00:20:11,677 --> 00:20:14,306
We both know this is
really what you want.

380
00:20:14,947 --> 00:20:16,939
Proof that you and
Walker are responsible

381
00:20:17,283 --> 00:20:18,581
for the destruction of Haven

382
00:20:18,784 --> 00:20:20,844
and the murder of
hundreds of innocent people.

383
00:20:25,691 --> 00:20:26,818
Now let her go.

384
00:20:27,526 --> 00:20:30,394
Let me guess, you made a copy

385
00:20:30,529 --> 00:20:32,930
so you can do this little
bit of theatrics for me.

386
00:20:33,299 --> 00:20:34,733
I expected more from you, Jim.

387
00:20:35,267 --> 00:20:39,227
Three soldiers against
one man without a gun.

388
00:20:41,440 --> 00:20:43,272
You sure you
brought enough help?

389
00:20:43,709 --> 00:20:44,904
I don't need any help.

390
00:20:46,412 --> 00:20:48,881
Is that why you sent
Nygma to do your dirty work?

391
00:20:49,515 --> 00:20:51,780
So he could pick up
your slack like I used to?

392
00:20:51,917 --> 00:20:55,479
Still dining out on pulling
me from that burning truck.

393
00:20:56,455 --> 00:20:57,923
I carried more
than my fair share.

394
00:20:58,257 --> 00:20:59,657
I was always the first man in.

395
00:20:59,825 --> 00:21:01,794
Your recklessness
got people killed.

396
00:21:01,994 --> 00:21:03,929
I don't know what the
hell you're talking about.

397
00:21:04,263 --> 00:21:05,263
Ramirez?

398
00:21:05,531 --> 00:21:06,260
No.

399
00:21:06,398 --> 00:21:07,398
Johnson.

400
00:21:07,800 --> 00:21:08,800
Bolton.

401
00:21:08,968 --> 00:21:10,596
Their deaths were not on me.

402
00:21:11,904 --> 00:21:12,904
Yeah.

403
00:21:14,039 --> 00:21:14,904
Put a bullet in my mouth

404
00:21:15,040 --> 00:21:16,406
and shut me up.

405
00:21:18,511 --> 00:21:19,511
No.

406
00:21:21,313 --> 00:21:22,474
Not a bullet.

407
00:21:23,048 --> 00:21:24,880
But I am gonna shut your mouth.

408
00:21:25,851 --> 00:21:27,012
Once and for all.

409
00:21:27,753 --> 00:21:29,278
Come try.

410
00:21:31,991 --> 00:21:34,859
(creaking)

411
00:21:35,628 --> 00:21:36,638
You sure this is the place?

412
00:21:36,662 --> 00:21:37,662
Yep.

413
00:21:38,464 --> 00:21:40,296
This is the place.

414
00:21:53,746 --> 00:21:54,770
Stop her!

415
00:21:56,782 --> 00:21:58,944
Ouchie. You're quick.

416
00:22:04,890 --> 00:22:06,381
I know you?

417
00:22:06,959 --> 00:22:09,326
- Doubt it.
- Uh-huh.

418
00:22:09,628 --> 00:22:12,689
You killed Jeremiah.
You're famous.

419
00:22:12,865 --> 00:22:14,993
(laughs) Not so scary.

420
00:22:15,334 --> 00:22:16,529
I could be famous.

421
00:22:16,769 --> 00:22:18,362
You want a shot at me, too?

422
00:22:18,504 --> 00:22:21,668
Unfortunately, you won't
have the opportunity.

423
00:22:22,608 --> 00:22:23,608
Careful.

424
00:22:23,843 --> 00:22:25,004
This is my workshop

425
00:22:25,511 --> 00:22:27,036
where I make my boom-booms.

426
00:22:27,379 --> 00:22:29,974
Anything here, boom,
boom. Then more boom

427
00:22:30,115 --> 00:22:32,084
and more boom,
then the whole room...

428
00:22:32,418 --> 00:22:35,718
- Boom, boom. We get it.
- You're bluffing.

429
00:22:35,855 --> 00:22:36,855
Catch.

430
00:22:37,857 --> 00:22:39,348
Ooh! No, no!

431
00:22:41,861 --> 00:22:44,888
- (watch ticking)
- MAGPIE: Whoops!

432
00:22:45,030 --> 00:22:48,023
(laughs) That watch
must have been an original,

433
00:22:48,400 --> 00:22:50,562
but other stuff in
there, boom, boom.

434
00:22:50,870 --> 00:22:52,031
Good luck.

435
00:23:03,048 --> 00:23:04,516
We tie our shoelaces together,

436
00:23:04,650 --> 00:23:06,585
then we lasso the
keys off the desk.

437
00:23:06,719 --> 00:23:10,087
And then what, Roy Rogers,
huh? They surrender in awe?

438
00:23:10,556 --> 00:23:12,957
No harebrained scheme
is gonna get us out of here.

439
00:23:14,159 --> 00:23:15,593
Hello!

440
00:23:16,829 --> 00:23:18,730
Hope you don't mind, I...

441
00:23:19,398 --> 00:23:22,459
borrowed some cop
stuff from the garage.

442
00:23:22,668 --> 00:23:24,830
Oh, boy, you can
put your guns down.

443
00:23:24,970 --> 00:23:27,769
Kevlar in this thing is
designed to withstand a blast

444
00:23:27,940 --> 00:23:30,171
that can tear down a
building twice this size.

445
00:23:30,776 --> 00:23:31,776
What is that?

446
00:23:33,412 --> 00:23:34,812
Is it not obvious?

447
00:23:35,014 --> 00:23:36,505
It's a bomb!

448
00:23:36,882 --> 00:23:39,078
Okay.

449
00:23:40,619 --> 00:23:41,985
Let's play a game.

450
00:23:45,457 --> 00:23:48,154
- Clear out.
- Ah, ah, ah, ah.

451
00:23:48,827 --> 00:23:51,126
If anyone leaves this
building, this thing blows.

452
00:23:52,631 --> 00:23:56,159
You gomers messed with
the wrong super genius,

453
00:23:56,535 --> 00:23:58,663
and now it's time
for a little payback.

454
00:23:58,804 --> 00:24:00,932
Nygma, you're a son of a bitch!

455
00:24:02,474 --> 00:24:04,534
Oh, not now, Bullock.

456
00:24:04,944 --> 00:24:07,539
The body count at Haven
wasn't big enough for you, huh?

457
00:24:07,680 --> 00:24:08,680
Shut up!

458
00:24:09,715 --> 00:24:10,842
What's your game?

459
00:24:10,983 --> 00:24:12,815
The only way to
deactivate this bomb

460
00:24:13,452 --> 00:24:16,115
is to put a four-digit
code on the keypad there.

461
00:24:16,555 --> 00:24:17,750
What's the code?

462
00:24:18,524 --> 00:24:19,787
(clears throat)

463
00:24:21,226 --> 00:24:25,721
The pallid beach mouse can
give birth every four weeks.

464
00:24:26,999 --> 00:24:27,762
Assuming we start

465
00:24:27,900 --> 00:24:28,959
with two mice,

466
00:24:29,101 --> 00:24:32,936
and that the size of an
average litter is 12 mice,

467
00:24:33,505 --> 00:24:36,737
and that they are equally
split male to female,

468
00:24:37,142 --> 00:24:39,702
how many pallid beach mice

469
00:24:39,845 --> 00:24:43,646
would we have at
the end of one year?

470
00:24:46,819 --> 00:24:49,983
The answer to that
question is the code.

471
00:24:51,557 --> 00:24:52,650
Oh, I almost forgot.

472
00:24:52,791 --> 00:24:56,091
- You have two minutes.
- (beeps)

473
00:24:56,595 --> 00:24:59,155
After that, everyone
in here goes splat.

474
00:25:00,866 --> 00:25:02,266
Except for me, of course.

475
00:25:05,070 --> 00:25:06,834
(panting)

476
00:25:18,517 --> 00:25:19,541
I'm in position.

477
00:25:21,720 --> 00:25:23,712
Copy. Plug the recorder
to the transmitter.

478
00:25:23,856 --> 00:25:25,767
Next, you'll connect the
transmitter to the radio.

479
00:25:25,791 --> 00:25:27,836
There should be a black
box at the base of the antenna.

480
00:25:27,860 --> 00:25:29,522
Let me know when
you get it open.

481
00:25:31,597 --> 00:25:33,775
- Nygma's bomb ploy
won't last forever.
- We just have to

482
00:25:33,799 --> 00:25:35,076
wait for the exhaust
fans to come to

483
00:25:35,100 --> 00:25:37,001
a complete stop before
you can use the gas.

484
00:25:53,819 --> 00:25:54,980
What are you doing?

485
00:25:55,554 --> 00:25:57,250
(grunting and groaning)

486
00:26:02,194 --> 00:26:03,787
(both grunting)

487
00:26:09,968 --> 00:26:12,096
(both groaning)

488
00:26:12,571 --> 00:26:16,565
You want to take
responsibility for people dying?

489
00:26:17,109 --> 00:26:18,600
Look around.

490
00:26:19,344 --> 00:26:20,642
This is all on you.

491
00:26:20,846 --> 00:26:22,075
What the hell does that mean?

492
00:26:22,614 --> 00:26:23,843
Walker spent months

493
00:26:24,083 --> 00:26:26,609
trying to convince her
superiors that Gotham

494
00:26:26,785 --> 00:26:28,344
was overrun by criminals.

495
00:26:29,054 --> 00:26:31,080
We were this close
to being sent in

496
00:26:31,924 --> 00:26:34,018
when you set up Haven.

497
00:26:34,259 --> 00:26:36,956
The fact that one man was able

498
00:26:37,096 --> 00:26:38,894
to safely house
and rescue hundreds

499
00:26:39,031 --> 00:26:41,125
of innocent civilians showed
the military higher-ups

500
00:26:41,233 --> 00:26:43,293
that the criminal
element of Gotham

501
00:26:43,635 --> 00:26:46,195
must not be so big
and scary after all.

502
00:26:47,906 --> 00:26:49,875
They were ready
to send in relief,

503
00:26:51,076 --> 00:26:52,840
so it had to come down.

504
00:26:54,113 --> 00:26:57,208
Why? What the
hell does she want?

505
00:26:57,850 --> 00:27:00,319
They were innocent
people, children.

506
00:27:00,986 --> 00:27:03,387
They were an acceptable
loss for the mission.

507
00:27:04,623 --> 00:27:05,784
What mission, damn it?

508
00:27:12,998 --> 00:27:14,125
(laughs)

509
00:27:21,640 --> 00:27:22,733
That's enough.

510
00:27:25,677 --> 00:27:26,269
(grunts)

511
00:27:26,411 --> 00:27:29,313
Take her out back and shoot her.

512
00:27:32,084 --> 00:27:34,144
He doesn't need to see this.

513
00:27:36,989 --> 00:27:38,048
It's my gift to you,

514
00:27:38,323 --> 00:27:39,723
a small mercy.

515
00:27:47,232 --> 00:27:49,312
You never should have
brought her into this, Eduardo.

516
00:27:49,668 --> 00:27:50,668
(groans)

517
00:28:04,149 --> 00:28:06,118
Can we speed things
along, please, Mr. Fox?

518
00:28:06,251 --> 00:28:07,913
The exhaust fans
have to wind down

519
00:28:08,053 --> 00:28:09,373
before they can
change directions.

520
00:28:09,688 --> 00:28:12,749
If you throw the canisters in
now, the gas will blow outside.

521
00:28:12,891 --> 00:28:14,792
Bruce, status?

522
00:28:16,828 --> 00:28:17,828
One second.

523
00:28:25,437 --> 00:28:27,372
I'm here. The box is open.

524
00:28:27,773 --> 00:28:29,284
LUCIUS: Two dark
green wires right in front.

525
00:28:29,308 --> 00:28:32,005
Attach those to the transmitter.
The message will send.

526
00:28:37,749 --> 00:28:40,776
(whirring, beeping)

527
00:28:41,320 --> 00:28:42,379
It's done.

528
00:28:47,926 --> 00:28:49,019
One minute left.

529
00:28:50,362 --> 00:28:51,489
Everybody hear that?

530
00:28:52,097 --> 00:28:54,760
One minute before
this thing explodes.

531
00:28:56,235 --> 00:28:57,235
I know the answer.

532
00:28:58,237 --> 00:29:01,401
Uh... (chuckles) Nope,
don't think you do, Bullock.

533
00:29:01,740 --> 00:29:04,471
My high school zoology class
is still rattling around up here.

534
00:29:04,810 --> 00:29:06,244
High school was a long time ago,

535
00:29:06,378 --> 00:29:09,075
so let's just take the
full minute, shall we?

536
00:29:09,481 --> 00:29:10,744
Really think about it.

537
00:29:10,882 --> 00:29:11,975
I don't need to.

538
00:29:12,317 --> 00:29:14,946
The pallid beach
mouse is extinct.

539
00:29:16,288 --> 00:29:19,122
It disappeared in 1959.

540
00:29:19,424 --> 00:29:22,292
The number you'll have at
the end of the year is zero.

541
00:29:22,461 --> 00:29:23,952
The number is zero.

542
00:29:24,396 --> 00:29:25,396
Trust me on this.

543
00:29:25,797 --> 00:29:27,797
I got in trouble. I had to
write an essay about it.

544
00:29:27,833 --> 00:29:29,062
I swear to God.

545
00:29:37,175 --> 00:29:38,336
Oh, dear.

546
00:29:45,951 --> 00:29:49,217
(indistinct chatter)

547
00:29:54,826 --> 00:29:55,826
(groans)

548
00:29:57,796 --> 00:29:59,025
I got a math problem for you.

549
00:29:59,197 --> 00:30:00,517
- Count these bullets.
- (inhales)

550
00:30:18,950 --> 00:30:19,950
Yes!

551
00:30:20,085 --> 00:30:21,576
- (grunts)
- You want to know why

552
00:30:21,920 --> 00:30:24,822
Ramirez and the
rest didn't make it out?

553
00:30:26,825 --> 00:30:28,418
After your tour of duty was up,

554
00:30:28,894 --> 00:30:30,385
we were caught
behind enemy lines,

555
00:30:30,829 --> 00:30:32,388
taken to the darkest
hell on Earth...

556
00:30:34,499 --> 00:30:35,933
Peña Duro.

557
00:30:36,301 --> 00:30:37,301
Have you heard of it?

558
00:30:37,336 --> 00:30:40,500
Prison? I didn't know.

559
00:30:41,406 --> 00:30:42,874
No one did,

560
00:30:43,875 --> 00:30:47,277
'cause it's where we were
left to die by our government.

561
00:30:48,313 --> 00:30:50,179
I was the only
one that survived.

562
00:30:50,582 --> 00:30:51,582
Walker...

563
00:30:52,117 --> 00:30:53,483
Walker, she found
me in that place.

564
00:30:54,086 --> 00:30:55,110
She pulled me out.

565
00:30:55,620 --> 00:30:57,213
She gave me a new purpose.

566
00:30:57,522 --> 00:30:58,581
She made you her killer.

567
00:30:58,957 --> 00:31:01,051
No, no, Jim.

568
00:31:02,227 --> 00:31:03,559
She's the killer.

569
00:31:04,129 --> 00:31:06,325
And you have no idea.

570
00:31:21,947 --> 00:31:24,439
- (gunshots)
- Lee?

571
00:31:43,502 --> 00:31:47,098
You and your stupid revenge.

572
00:31:47,539 --> 00:31:50,304
The diamond isn't even worth
anything in Gotham anyway.

573
00:31:53,412 --> 00:31:55,005
Unless...

574
00:31:58,350 --> 00:32:01,912
The diamond is worth
something on the mainland.

575
00:32:02,421 --> 00:32:04,515
And I bet you have

576
00:32:05,357 --> 00:32:08,350
way more where that came from.

577
00:32:12,631 --> 00:32:13,929
You're leaving Gotham.

578
00:32:17,936 --> 00:32:20,371
Congratulations,
you're a genius.

579
00:32:21,373 --> 00:32:22,373
Why?

580
00:32:23,542 --> 00:32:25,374
"Alexander the Great

581
00:32:25,677 --> 00:32:28,010
"then looked upon his land

582
00:32:28,447 --> 00:32:29,642
"and wept.

583
00:32:30,048 --> 00:32:33,143
For there was nothing
left to conquer."

584
00:32:36,188 --> 00:32:38,157
I've done all I can in Gotham.

585
00:32:39,491 --> 00:32:41,255
Some things twice.
(chuckles lightly)

586
00:32:42,994 --> 00:32:45,054
There's nothing left
for me here anymore.

587
00:32:47,399 --> 00:32:48,560
I want in.

588
00:32:50,669 --> 00:32:53,639
I can't even walk
around the city

589
00:32:53,972 --> 00:32:56,203
without some lowlife
trying to take me out.

590
00:32:56,441 --> 00:32:58,307
Just so he can up his cred.

591
00:32:59,077 --> 00:33:01,672
I don't want to be the person
who killed Jeremiah Valeska.

592
00:33:04,115 --> 00:33:05,606
I just want to be myself.

593
00:33:08,086 --> 00:33:10,214
And I can't do that
in Gotham anymore.

594
00:33:11,623 --> 00:33:13,387
My heart weeps for you.

595
00:33:15,260 --> 00:33:16,353
But no.

596
00:33:18,530 --> 00:33:21,227
- What if I could get us
out of here?
- (Oswald laughs)

597
00:33:21,366 --> 00:33:23,198
You do that, I will cut you in.

598
00:33:23,568 --> 00:33:24,568
50-50.

599
00:33:25,036 --> 00:33:28,768
Ten percent, and
I won't murder you

600
00:33:29,107 --> 00:33:31,474
- once we reach
the mainland.
- Deal.

601
00:33:33,245 --> 00:33:34,372
Selina, wait!

602
00:33:38,683 --> 00:33:44,145
She said, "Anything in
the room could be a bomb."

603
00:33:44,356 --> 00:33:46,257
Not the room itself.

604
00:33:47,158 --> 00:33:48,592
Did you know that
the whole time?

605
00:33:49,094 --> 00:33:50,118
Just thought of it.

606
00:33:51,530 --> 00:33:52,259
We should hurry before

607
00:33:52,397 --> 00:33:53,677
she gets away
with all our stuff.

608
00:33:54,165 --> 00:33:55,165
Don't worry.

609
00:33:56,034 --> 00:33:57,502
She won't get far.

610
00:33:59,704 --> 00:34:02,538
Alvarez, take Lee to my office.

611
00:34:02,807 --> 00:34:03,807
Be right there.

612
00:34:04,776 --> 00:34:06,574
I got to be honest
with you, man.

613
00:34:07,245 --> 00:34:09,056
I don't know how many
more of these close calls

614
00:34:09,080 --> 00:34:10,309
this old heart can take.

615
00:34:10,448 --> 00:34:12,008
We were able to
transmit the audio logs,

616
00:34:12,050 --> 00:34:13,494
but until I hear back
from my contacts,

617
00:34:13,518 --> 00:34:14,795
I won't know if
they received them.

618
00:34:14,819 --> 00:34:16,253
Well, if they did,

619
00:34:16,521 --> 00:34:17,819
probably have a
lot of questions.

620
00:34:18,823 --> 00:34:19,688
We'll spend the
night and help out.

621
00:34:19,824 --> 00:34:22,103
Alfred, can you go back to the
apartment and grab what you can?

622
00:34:22,127 --> 00:34:23,425
Certainly, Master Bruce.

623
00:34:24,462 --> 00:34:25,486
Thanks, Bruce.

624
00:34:29,134 --> 00:34:30,454
The next time I
get a short straw,

625
00:34:30,535 --> 00:34:31,815
I'm just gonna
walk into traffic.

626
00:34:32,437 --> 00:34:34,497
Where has Lee
been this whole time?

627
00:34:35,674 --> 00:34:36,698
I don't know.

628
00:34:37,409 --> 00:34:38,502
I'm not gonna wait around

629
00:34:38,643 --> 00:34:40,669
to watch their touching reunion.

630
00:34:49,788 --> 00:34:51,723
What is happening here?

631
00:34:53,191 --> 00:34:54,659
I don't know where to start.

632
00:34:55,727 --> 00:34:57,559
When I didn't hear from you,

633
00:34:58,330 --> 00:35:00,299
I'd hoped you'd
gotten out of Gotham.

634
00:35:01,700 --> 00:35:03,726
The last thing that I remember

635
00:35:04,536 --> 00:35:07,096
is Jeremiah
destroying the bridges.

636
00:35:07,672 --> 00:35:09,140
That was over three months ago.

637
00:35:09,274 --> 00:35:11,573
Three months? Oh, my God.

638
00:35:12,277 --> 00:35:13,397
You don't remember anything?

639
00:35:14,179 --> 00:35:15,579
I'm trying, and...

640
00:35:17,315 --> 00:35:19,307
Right before the
bridges came down,

641
00:35:19,884 --> 00:35:21,512
you said you were
leaving the city.

642
00:35:22,454 --> 00:35:23,183
Why didn't you?

643
00:35:23,455 --> 00:35:24,252
I don't know, Jim.

644
00:35:24,389 --> 00:35:26,119
None of this is
making any sense.

645
00:35:28,593 --> 00:35:29,891
We're still cut off.

646
00:35:31,296 --> 00:35:32,559
There's no help.

647
00:35:33,665 --> 00:35:34,724
It's been hard.

648
00:35:36,701 --> 00:35:38,602
Actually, that's not true.

649
00:35:39,804 --> 00:35:41,136
It's been hell.

650
00:35:42,507 --> 00:35:43,531
People needed hope,

651
00:35:43,675 --> 00:35:45,473
so I put on a brave face.

652
00:35:46,578 --> 00:35:48,137
Pretended I knew what to do.

653
00:35:49,514 --> 00:35:50,846
But you know me, Lee.

654
00:35:51,783 --> 00:35:52,783
I'm just a cop.

655
00:35:56,721 --> 00:35:58,747
And now I'm really lost.

656
00:35:59,858 --> 00:36:01,258
Hey, it's okay.

657
00:36:01,393 --> 00:36:03,157
(distant siren)

658
00:36:03,595 --> 00:36:05,530
We're here now.

659
00:36:06,464 --> 00:36:09,866
We'll just try to put the
pieces back together again.

660
00:36:10,769 --> 00:36:12,260
Maybe we can help each other.

661
00:36:15,273 --> 00:36:16,798
Let's start with my memory.

662
00:36:19,778 --> 00:36:21,406
I went back to the Narrows.

663
00:36:22,814 --> 00:36:24,248
I saw Ed.

664
00:36:24,949 --> 00:36:27,248
We fought. We...

665
00:36:28,853 --> 00:36:29,853
Oh.

666
00:36:30,889 --> 00:36:31,948
He stabbed me.

667
00:36:32,891 --> 00:36:34,860
- What?
- Wait, let me
remember.

668
00:36:35,427 --> 00:36:36,759
I stabbed him.

669
00:36:38,263 --> 00:36:39,959
There was so much blood.

670
00:36:42,233 --> 00:36:45,567
Then I was... (exhales)

671
00:36:45,704 --> 00:36:47,263
somewhere...

672
00:36:48,840 --> 00:36:50,468
A hospital, maybe?

673
00:37:11,463 --> 00:37:14,365
- (scoffs)
- (man groans)

674
00:37:15,500 --> 00:37:18,493
Eduardo. (clicks tongue)

675
00:37:18,636 --> 00:37:20,366
So much potential,

676
00:37:20,505 --> 00:37:22,770
but not quite up to the task.

677
00:37:23,007 --> 00:37:24,270
- (beeping)
- Walker.

678
00:37:24,409 --> 00:37:25,707
(case opens)

679
00:37:25,944 --> 00:37:26,944
How?

680
00:37:27,645 --> 00:37:28,908
We've been burned.

681
00:37:29,714 --> 00:37:30,954
The press have recordings of me

682
00:37:31,249 --> 00:37:33,582
instructing Nygma
to take down Haven.

683
00:37:34,486 --> 00:37:36,250
I thought I might
accomplish my task

684
00:37:36,387 --> 00:37:38,413
without setting
foot in this foul city,

685
00:37:38,556 --> 00:37:40,923
but that's not to be.

686
00:37:42,994 --> 00:37:45,725
- You won't die yet,
my warrior.
- (groaning)

687
00:37:46,598 --> 00:37:48,999
There is so much
more for you to do.

688
00:37:49,634 --> 00:37:52,468
Professor Strange will
have you fixed up in no time.

689
00:37:54,038 --> 00:37:55,904
(device beeps, clicks)

690
00:37:56,541 --> 00:37:57,804
(respirator powers up)

691
00:38:00,879 --> 00:38:02,507
(sharp inhale)

692
00:38:02,647 --> 00:38:04,639
(distorted): Jim Gordon.

693
00:38:05,450 --> 00:38:07,316
Don't worry about Gordon.

694
00:38:07,786 --> 00:38:09,755
Nygma was never alone.

695
00:38:11,055 --> 00:38:13,354
There was a second operative.

696
00:38:16,461 --> 00:38:17,929
(sharp inhale)

697
00:38:18,930 --> 00:38:19,930
You okay?

698
00:38:20,398 --> 00:38:21,398
You cold?

699
00:38:24,435 --> 00:38:25,698
(brief whir)

700
00:38:26,638 --> 00:38:28,539
The hospitals were
all out of commission.

701
00:38:28,873 --> 00:38:30,808
Just the one clinic
in the green zone.

702
00:38:36,781 --> 00:38:37,781
Lee?

703
00:38:37,816 --> 00:38:39,375
Time to die, Jim Gordon.

704
00:38:40,585 --> 00:38:41,917
(Jim grunting)

705
00:38:43,855 --> 00:38:46,654
(both grunting)

706
00:38:51,095 --> 00:38:53,963
- (electricity crackles)
- (exhales)

707
00:39:03,775 --> 00:39:05,835
(owls hooting)

708
00:39:12,016 --> 00:39:14,349
(faint clanging)

709
00:39:21,459 --> 00:39:23,428
(leaves rustling)

710
00:39:29,767 --> 00:39:31,736
♪♪

711
00:39:44,148 --> 00:39:46,447
- (grunts, groans)
- Ah...

712
00:39:46,584 --> 00:39:47,711
(thud)

713
00:39:51,055 --> 00:39:53,923
Wanted: one butler.

714
00:39:55,126 --> 00:39:57,095
Military background a plus.

715
00:39:57,528 --> 00:39:59,690
References required.

716
00:40:00,865 --> 00:40:03,926
Yes, Mr. Pennyworth.

717
00:40:04,602 --> 00:40:06,867
I have a job for you.

718
00:40:07,939 --> 00:40:09,965
(distant foghorn)

719
00:40:14,178 --> 00:40:15,476
(rapid gasping)

720
00:40:15,647 --> 00:40:17,024
SELINA: Well, you
weren't lying when you said

721
00:40:17,048 --> 00:40:18,414
she wouldn't get far.

722
00:40:18,716 --> 00:40:19,979
(dog panting)

723
00:40:21,152 --> 00:40:24,554
Pretty crown's...
(chuckles) booby-trapped.

724
00:40:25,056 --> 00:40:26,547
Ouch.

725
00:40:26,891 --> 00:40:28,450
Ouch, indeed.

726
00:40:29,427 --> 00:40:30,771
I couldn't have you
coming back here

727
00:40:30,795 --> 00:40:32,855
and making off with
all of my belongings.

728
00:40:33,598 --> 00:40:36,067
Our belongings.

729
00:40:36,768 --> 00:40:39,670
Cut my wings, let me go?

730
00:40:42,040 --> 00:40:43,040
Bang, bang.

731
00:40:44,509 --> 00:40:45,135
- What the hell?
- (dog whines)

732
00:40:45,476 --> 00:40:47,945
The buckshot in her gut
wasn't enough punishment?

733
00:40:48,513 --> 00:40:49,993
Just a little reminder
of what happens

734
00:40:50,048 --> 00:40:51,914
when people try
to steal from me.

735
00:40:52,050 --> 00:40:54,918
(shuddering breath)
Good to know.

736
00:40:57,522 --> 00:40:58,546
Partner.

737
00:40:58,823 --> 00:41:00,689
(sirens wailing)

738
00:41:03,661 --> 00:41:05,095
BULLOCK: So Lee has a chip, too?

739
00:41:05,730 --> 00:41:07,824
GORDON: I don't know
how, but it seems that way.

740
00:41:08,499 --> 00:41:09,944
Walker must have
been holding onto her

741
00:41:09,968 --> 00:41:11,834
as some sort of plan
B this whole time.

742
00:41:11,970 --> 00:41:14,804
Three months? That's a hell
of a long game she's playing.

743
00:41:15,006 --> 00:41:17,840
Which means she probably
has other tricks up her sleeve.

744
00:41:18,509 --> 00:41:19,509
(moans)

745
00:41:20,712 --> 00:41:21,712
Hey.

746
00:41:24,182 --> 00:41:25,241
(grunts)

747
00:41:25,717 --> 00:41:27,242
- Welcome back.
- Oh, hell.

748
00:41:27,585 --> 00:41:29,144
This is gonna keep happening.

749
00:41:30,655 --> 00:41:31,918
What's wrong with me?

750
00:41:33,124 --> 00:41:35,218
The good news is, I think
I can actually answer that.

751
00:41:35,760 --> 00:41:37,524
- (exhales)
- (door opens)

752
00:41:37,662 --> 00:41:38,662
BARBARA: Jim.

753
00:41:39,831 --> 00:41:40,924
I need to talk to you.

754
00:41:41,199 --> 00:41:43,031
Now is not a good time, Barbara.

755
00:41:44,569 --> 00:41:46,613
Don't you remember the good
time we had the other night?

756
00:41:46,637 --> 00:41:48,037
- Over there?
- Say what?

757
00:41:48,272 --> 00:41:49,763
You need to leave now.

758
00:41:50,274 --> 00:41:51,833
I'm pregnant.

759
00:41:55,013 --> 00:41:56,504
Good to see you, Lee.

760
00:41:57,181 --> 00:41:58,581
Long time.

761
00:42:06,624 --> 00:42:07,990
Congratulations?

762
00:42:11,596 --> 00:42:12,859
(buoy bell dinging)

763
00:42:14,232 --> 00:42:15,256
(labored breathing)

764
00:42:18,736 --> 00:42:19,760
You.

765
00:42:20,538 --> 00:42:22,063
You're supposed
to be dead, mate.

766
00:42:22,874 --> 00:42:24,809
Sorry to disappoint.

767
00:42:26,044 --> 00:42:28,104
You hurt my boy...

768
00:42:29,013 --> 00:42:31,175
and I will bite your face off.

769
00:42:31,315 --> 00:42:33,307
(manic giggling)

770
00:42:33,651 --> 00:42:35,313
(laughing): Yeah. Yeah.

771
00:42:36,287 --> 00:42:40,190
Ah... Bruce will
be along shortly.

772
00:42:40,992 --> 00:42:42,620
In the meantime...

773
00:42:45,696 --> 00:42:47,324
This place is in desperate need

774
00:42:47,665 --> 00:42:50,134
of some good,
old-fashioned butlering.

775
00:42:53,638 --> 00:42:56,005
How did you even find a way
off the bloody island anyway?

776
00:42:56,307 --> 00:42:59,300
How? One if by land,

777
00:42:59,777 --> 00:43:00,938
two if by sea,

778
00:43:01,312 --> 00:43:04,111
three if by digging a
tunnel under the river.

779
00:43:04,315 --> 00:43:07,080
But enough questions.

780
00:43:10,288 --> 00:43:12,723
Today is the big day.
